WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:05.670
The year was 1994, and all seemed well at
Site-19.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:11.510
Researchers, administrative staff, containment
specialists, and guards saw to their daily

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:12.519
tasks.

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:17.689
As the largest SCP Foundation containment
site on the books, there was something paradoxically

00:00:17.689 --> 00:00:22.470
prestigious and pedestrian about working at
the legendary facility.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:27.769
Of course, among staff at the SCP Foundation,
the Site was famous and took on a certain

00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:32.140
mythology around break-room discussions for
those who weren’t assigned there.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.440
“Did you see the crazy new tech they got
at Site-19?

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.520
It’s like something out of Star Wars.”

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.460
“I heard some of the anomalies they store
at Site-19 can cause XK-Class End of the World

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.760
Scenarios, so they’ve got Hammer Down on
speed dial.”

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.260
“My buddy told me that Site-19 has a pool
on the fifth floor.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.860
Everyone who works there gets to go up and
chill there once a week, and they’ve got

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.940
a dedicated bar up there that serves cocktails
with the little umbrellas all night long!”

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.650
Of course, some rumors are truer than others
- Not the pool thing, though.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.970
Sorry, that one’s complete baloney.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:10.380
It is, however, completely true of the SCP
Explained Office, but hey, that’s a story

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.140
for another day.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.500
Site-19, as a result of its size, does indeed
need a simultaneously large and elite staff

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.230
to handle its many unique containment requirements.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:26.110
People work around the clock to ensure everything
runs smoothly, knowing that the risk of dropping

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.560
the ball means death and destruction on a
wide scale.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.340
Personnel have been trained to meticulously
study new anomalies, discover their strengths

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:41.210
and weaknesses, drum up ideal containment
requirements, and get the situation on lock

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.980
as quickly as possible.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:48.310
Most of the time, the operations at Site-19
run smoother than George Benson.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.710
Most of the time.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:55.340
Of course, with the exception of a few researchers
there - Sorry, Dr. Bright and Dr. Crow - everyone

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.480
who works at Site-19 is only human.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:04.090
Mistakes can happen, even when the cost of
those mistakes is much costlier than when

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:10.200
you forget to carry the zero on your dull
office job’s latest mind-numbing spreadsheet.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:16.250
Little oversights can pile up into catastrophes
over time, especially when you put your mind

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:22.810
to committing the greatest sin of all: Underestimating
your opponent, exactly as the SCP Foundation

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.090
did one fateful day in 1994.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:32.670
You see, the SCP Foundation contains hundreds,
if not thousands of new anomalies every single

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.670
year.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:38.450
When this job becomes your daily bread and
butter, what once seemed exceptional slowly

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.310
becomes humdrum and mundane.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.610
Just like the earlier rumors suggested, there
are monsters, entities, and objects locked

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:52.410
up at Site-19 that could cause an XK-Class
End of the World Scenario if they ever got

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:53.410
out.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.840
Could the fine folks staffing that site really
be blamed for not paying that much attention

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.790
to some anomalous sculpture?

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:05.770
They just weren’t aware how incredibly dire
the consequences of taking your eyes off of

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:13.450
SCP-173 were yet, but believe me when I say
they were about to receive a hell of an education.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:19.460
The Sculpture had come into their care a year
earlier in 1993, though the exact circumstances

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:24.180
of how it came to Site-19 tend to differ depending
on who you ask.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.950
Its anomalous ability had become apparent
rather quickly, at the expense of a few careless

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.520
personnel’s lives - But hey, isn’t that
always the way these things unfold?

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.629
The Sculpture could be frozen in place by
looking at it, though whenever people turned

00:03:38.629 --> 00:03:44.520
their gaze away, it moved towards its prey
at shocking speeds and killed them by either

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.680
strangulation or, more commonly, snapping
necks.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.620
Sure, it wasn’t the kind of entity you’d
want to have a cup of coffee with or place

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.260
in your living room as a striking new Objet
D’Art, but the Foundation had plenty of

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.220
these so-called “Murder Monsters” in containment
already.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:06.239
And this was one you didn’t even need to
shoot - Just looking at it is enough to freeze

00:04:06.239 --> 00:04:07.540
it in place.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:11.660
A few D-Classes were lost during the first
few months of figuring out the best way to

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:16.870
clean the nasty creature’s cell - on account
of the fact that it poops constantly - but

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:20.549
other than that it was all pretty smooth sailing.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:25.810
They had no idea just how dangerous this work
of malicious modern art was.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:34.729
This brings us back to the fateful day in
1994: The day when SCP-173 first breached

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:35.860
containment.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:41.300
Researcher Theo Trask was walking down the
long, winding halls of Site-19, marking off

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.770
each successfully contained anomaly on his
clipboard.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.289
It was a monotonous job, but somebody had
to do it.

00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:52.219
He looked through the viewing windows of all
the anomalies that it wasn’t deadly to look

00:04:52.219 --> 00:04:54.069
at, taking inventory.

00:04:54.069 --> 00:04:59.569
He’d worked at the SCP Foundation for eight
years now, and nothing amazed him anymore.

00:04:59.569 --> 00:05:03.169
He was thinking about what he might have for
dinner that night, or whether he should go

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:06.229
to the movie theater to see that new film,
Pulp Fiction.

00:05:06.229 --> 00:05:12.229
Up in the security office for Sector C of
Site-19, Security Officer Todd Buffett and

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:15.509
his men observed the monitors glowing before
them.

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:18.150
It was a blissfully boring day.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.250
One of them was discreetly playing Snake on
his Nokia under the table.

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:28.000
Days like this were made for wasting, and
they were tragically few at the SCP Foundation

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.360
- Where being excited was often the prelude
for being dead.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.919
Todd yawned and was happy for the privilege
to do so.

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:41.220
In a darkened Sector C server room, a group
of engineers performed routine maintenance.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.279
They could see their breath; these rooms were
kept extra cold to prevent the technology

00:05:45.279 --> 00:05:46.659
from overheating.

00:05:46.659 --> 00:05:50.469
It was intricate work, but they were well-trained
to do it.

00:05:50.469 --> 00:05:54.939
So much so that they sometimes slipped into
a state of auto-pilot while conducting these

00:05:54.939 --> 00:06:00.780
checks, saving the burden of conscious thought
for meatier tasks further down the line.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:06.839
These were unremarkable tasks on an unremarkable
day, and they would be happy for it all to

00:06:06.839 --> 00:06:07.839
end.

00:06:07.839 --> 00:06:12.389
And of course, it would, just not in the sense
that they meant.

00:06:12.389 --> 00:06:16.879
A mixed group of researchers and administrative
staff sat in the breakroom, enjoying their

00:06:16.879 --> 00:06:21.729
mediocre packed lunch and sharing their own
version of inane office banter.

00:06:21.729 --> 00:06:26.080
It was nice to step away from the often-deadly
grind and shoot the breeze with others in

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.619
their line of work.

00:06:27.619 --> 00:06:32.849
After all, it was a strange job with its own
unique pressures - You needed to discuss it

00:06:32.849 --> 00:06:38.259
with others who got it sometimes just to avoid
going insane from the pressure.

00:06:38.259 --> 00:06:43.050
But then, you needed to avoid the unforgiving
blade of this double-edged sword’s mirror

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:48.819
half: You never wanted to get too attached
to a co-worker at the SCP Foundation, because

00:06:48.819 --> 00:06:52.889
who knows, you might not be seeing them again
tomorrow.

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:56.240
Or they might not be seeing you.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.469
Back in the server bank, a junior technician
named Colin Muntz was about to make one teeny,

00:07:01.469 --> 00:07:02.840
tiny little mistake.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:08.669
While running diagnostics on one of the room’s
power cores, he tripped a fuse and set a terrible

00:07:08.669 --> 00:07:11.069
chain reaction in motion.

00:07:11.069 --> 00:07:15.740
Cables running all through Site-19 caught
the shockwaves of Colin’s poor decision

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:20.289
in a moment, causing a series of power cuts
that ran through different systems throughout

00:07:20.289 --> 00:07:26.180
the facility, each one tripping the next like
the proverbial series of tumbling dominos.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:30.080
Back to Researcher Theo Trask, lazily ticking
boxes on his clipboard.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.879
He was about to do another one and break for
lunch when the lights overhead suddenly went

00:07:34.879 --> 00:07:37.379
out, leaving him in a dark hallway.

00:07:37.379 --> 00:07:41.929
He was immediately broken from his trance,
heart beating twenty to the dozen.

00:07:41.929 --> 00:07:44.529
Seconds passed and the dark somehow remained.

00:07:44.529 --> 00:07:46.490
What was going on here?

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.089
It didn’t make sense.

00:07:48.089 --> 00:07:51.340
Surely the auxiliary generator should have
kicked in by now, right?

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.909
And on any other day, it would have.

00:07:53.909 --> 00:07:59.219
But unluckily for Theo Trask, this just so
happened to be the one day in the last several

00:07:59.219 --> 00:08:05.419
months that the facility’s main backup generator
was also experiencing routine maintenance.

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:08.639
Sometimes, life just isn’t fair.

00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:13.479
Trask felt his heart leap up into his throat
as a door somewhere behind him slowly creaked

00:08:13.479 --> 00:08:16.869
open with a torturously long, metallic screech.

00:08:16.869 --> 00:08:22.030
Trask didn’t have a gun, or even a taser,
but he did have a small flashlight attached

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:23.030
to his belt.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:27.569
With draw speed that would rival Clint Eastwood
in his prime, the terrified Trask whipped

00:08:27.569 --> 00:08:31.459
out his flashlight and shone it in the direction
of the noise.

00:08:31.459 --> 00:08:36.099
His beam caught a static figure, staring at
him without a face.

00:08:36.099 --> 00:08:41.910
The rigid, soulless body of the Sculpture,
SCP-173, was standing less than ten feet away

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:42.950
from him.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:48.390
In this light, its smears of Krylon-brand
spray paint looked almost like a mocking grin.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.280
Your move, researcher, it seemed to say.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.120
Trask had broken into a cold sweat.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:59.070
His hand was shaking, but he needed to keep
the beam on that monster.

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:02.700
Whatever tripped the power must have compromised
the system that governed the Sculpture’s

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.800
electrical deadbolt, and unless the lightbulbs
came back on and the hallway was suddenly

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:11.070
flooded with other personnel and a forklift
to get that miserable chunk of concrete back

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.950
into its cell, then he was in real trouble.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:18.430
He couldn’t run - It would kill him the
second he turned around.

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:22.010
And he couldn’t walk backwards - one trip
in the dark and he’d be found dead on the

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:25.220
ground, head facing in the wrong direction.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:27.170
All he could do now was hope.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:30.150
Hope, and of course, not blink.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:35.580
But as the seconds passed and the pressure
mounted, this became harder and harder to

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:36.580
achieve.

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:41.490
He could feel the surface of his eyes drying
out, leading to a sharp stinging sensation.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:47.090
Still, the Sculpture lingered, and waited,
as Researcher Trask’s eyes began to water.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:50.000
People should be coming, shouldn’t they?

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:51.610
Surely help should be on the way?

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:55.810
But he couldn’t hear footsteps or the squeaking
tires of a forklift just yet.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:10:01.562
He couldn’t hear anything except his heartbeat
and the blood rushing in his ears.

00:10:01.562 --> 00:10:06.690
His eyes were killing him - Just metaphorically
for now, but not for long.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.090
What harm would one little blink do?

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.110
Only for a fraction of a second…

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:15.480
Now, before we proceed to the researcher’s
gruesome and terrifying death, we’d like

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.680
to perform a quick experiment with you, the
viewer of SCP Explained.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.420
We’ve created a number of videos exploring
the fascinating and frightening enigma of

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:30.720
SCP-173, and one statement seems to pop up
in the comments again and again from back-seat

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:32.110
containment experts:

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:35.430
“Why not just blink your eyes one at a time?

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.110
That’s a surefire way to keep it locked
into place.”

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:41.070
Okay, let’s give that a try.

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.520
Open both your eyes nice and wide.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.900
Now blink your left eye, now blink your right.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:51.130
Left, right, left, right.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.020
Keep going while I’m talking.

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.050
Don’t stop.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:55.050
Keep it up.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:56.150
How are your eyes feeling?

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:59.330
It makes your vision a little blurry, doesn’t
it?

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:02.360
Kind of makes your eyes sting a little as
time goes on.

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.100
Oh, look who it is…

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.060
Well, if it isn’t SCP-173.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.890
What a surprise!

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:12.430
And it looks very interested in you, doesn’t
it?

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:15.780
Better keep one of your eyes open at all times.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.430
Left blink, right blink, left blink, right
blink.

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:24.510
No pressure, of course, but if you’ve closed
your eyes at any time during this little experiment,

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:27.950
you’re already laying dead on the ground
with a broken neck.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.760
Left, right, left, right.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:32.060
Do you feel the burn yet?

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.550
There’s no shame in giving up.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:37.470
All it’ll cost you is your life.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:41.950
And that’s why we don’t blink one eye
at a time.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.680
Back to the story.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.060
Researcher Trask couldn’t take it any longer.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:50.860
His aching eyes fluttered closed for an almost
imperceptible moment of time, and that was

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:53.060
all this sinister Sculpture needed.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:57.490
Trask felt the impossibly cold concrete grip
of its stubby arms on either side of head,

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:03.250
and a sharp jerk that communicated a mere
moment of unimaginable pain followed by the

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.200
physical answer to silence.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:06.200
His body went slack underneath him and his
thoughts drained, his head twisted at an utterly

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:07.200
unnatural angle.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:08.200
The Sculpture dropped Trask’s limp corpse
onto the ground and darted through the darkness

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.300
of the hallway, with no living eyes left to
freeze it in place.

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:13.830
In the darkness, it was impossible to defeat.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:18.200
It would do the two things it knew how to
do: Hunt and murder.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.860
In the Sector C security office, all hell
was breaking loose.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:28.590
Thanks to Colin Muntz’s little oopsie in
the server room, all the monitors were dark,

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:33.440
and the corded phones that gave them a direct
line to assistance were all dead.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.880
They had no idea what was happening out there.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.080
Maybe they’d gotten lucky and none of the
cells were open.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:41.850
Maybe they’d gotten extremely unlucky and
ALL of the cells were opened.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:46.290
Whatever caused this catastrophic power failure
must have also knocked out the alarm system,

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.200
because they were getting nothing.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:53.150
When the door behind Security Officer Todd
Buffett and his men opened, he was profoundly

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:56.140
relieved to think that assistance had already
arrived.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:13:00.940
He and his men were a lot less relieved when
they fall fell to the ground, their necks

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:06.070
snapped with exquisite efficiency - if you’re
into that kind of thing.

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:11.110
That’s because The Sculpture had found its
way into the security office and gave itself

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:16.320
more crunch than you’d get from a family-sized
bag of Doritos, but with these new victims

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:20.860
now little more than dead meat, it was time
to move on.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:24.540
Over in the break room, the collection of
research and administration staff who were

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:28.490
enjoying pleasant chitchat not long ago were
now hidden away in darkness.

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:33.260
It was so bleak in there that they couldn’t
see the cup ramen steaming inches away from

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:34.520
their faces.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:40.380
In moments like this, the usual advice is
to stay calm and wait for the situation to

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:41.840
be resolved.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:46.860
After all, if you tried to take anything into
your own hands, you might fall over and injure

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:47.860
yourself.

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:49.750
Normally, this is good advice.

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:54.370
It’s less effective when the thing that
wishes you grave injury is actively on its

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:58.300
way, and sitting in the dark certainly isn’t
going to stop it.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:02.380
Elsewhere in the building, the engineering
time were scrambling like there was no tomorrow

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:04.050
to rectify their mistake.

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:09.540
Partly because, if SCP-173 got to them before
they’d gotten the lights working again,

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.150
there really would be no tomorrow.

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:16.110
With their mini flashlights, they desperately
searched for any way to rectify the issue

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:18.380
and get everything turned back on.

00:14:18.380 --> 00:14:23.710
In a normal company, the biggest concern in
a situation like this might be massive irretrievable

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:24.870
data loss.

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:30.230
But of course, at the SCP Foundation, the
issue is more a massive and irretrievable

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.310
loss of human life.

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:34.170
And also the data, too.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:39.840
A few miles south of Site-19, a different
kind of cavalry was on its way: A detachment

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:45.100
of MTF Nu-7, Hammer Down, were on their way
in a pair of armored helicopters.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:48.900
Their assault rifles had flashlights fixed
to them and their helmets came fitted with

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.290
night vision goggles as standard.

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:56.600
Their intel suggested a large portion of Site-19’s
Sector C had gone dark for whatever reason,

00:14:56.600 --> 00:15:00.120
and they were ready to drop in and get things
back under control.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:04.470
The one question was whether they’d get
there in time to save the people in the break

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:08.520
room, as the Sculpture drew ever closer to
their location.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:14.170
Speaking of, the group of research and administration
staff were still just sitting around, waiting

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:16.380
for the lights to turn back on.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:21.650
One of the more senior researchers in the
room, Dr. Sherry Brewis, was a lifelong smoker,

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:26.370
and as such, she was fishing around in her
purse for her Zippo lighter to shed some light

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:27.370
on the situation.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:32.200
Her rummaging was so loud that she could barely
even hear the door to the room beginning to

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:33.830
creak open.

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:39.610
But she heard the first snap, and the awful,
animalistic chorus of screams that followed.

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:40.610
Crunch.

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:41.610
Crunch.

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:42.610
Crunch.

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:46.940
As each of her fellow members of Foundation
personnel fell, she could feel the thing killing

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:48.560
them getting closer.

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:53.250
She could almost feel the same icy limbs gripping
her skull when she whipped out and lit the

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:58.190
Zippo, the flame coming alive in front of
her face with that pleasing metallic click.

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:03.190
It didn’t offer much light, just enough
for her to see the monster looming inches

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:09.790
away from her face: SCP-173, limbs outstretched
and ready.

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:11.690
The rest of the room was silent.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:15.290
She knew, in that moment, she was the only
one alive in here now.

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:18.180
But she still wasn’t the only survivor yet.

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:21.030
That all depended on what happened next.

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:26.370
The flame danced and the Sculpture remained
frozen in place, standing so close to her.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:27.670
It wanted her.

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:29.960
It wanted to hear her neck crunch.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:34.960
The flames began to dance erratically and
Dr. Brewis felt her heart rate spike.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:38.930
One careless breath, and the light keeping
her alive would be gone.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:43.650
But even if she did everything right here,
it might not be enough to save her.

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:48.060
It didn’t even need a second to do what
it wanted so badly to do.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.680
The light seemed almost to flicker.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:52.690
Getting smaller, getting weaker.

00:16:52.690 --> 00:16:55.270
Dr. Brewis felt tears dribbling down her cheeks.

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:56.270
Any second now.

00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:57.430
Any second now…

00:16:57.430 --> 00:16:58.430
Click.

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:00.290
The lights above her turned back on.

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:04.409
She could have screamed in delirious relief
- But that might also have been a scream at

00:17:04.409 --> 00:17:08.569
the expense of her many dead coworkers, littered
around the room.

00:17:08.569 --> 00:17:13.189
Either way, her thoughts were interrupted
by a staccato burst of footsteps, followed

00:17:13.189 --> 00:17:16.660
by the BOOM of the door being kicked in by
a booted foot.

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:19.439
The members of Hammer Down were standing in
the doorway.

00:17:19.439 --> 00:17:20.669
She was saved.

00:17:20.669 --> 00:17:25.409
In the hours that followed, things were righted,
as best they could be with so many deaths.

00:17:25.409 --> 00:17:30.129
The Sculpture was returned to containment,
the bodies were cleared away, the electrical

00:17:30.129 --> 00:17:31.730
issues were fixed.

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:37.100
Much would continue on as normal after that
day - death, after all, was an occupational

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:44.610
hazard for those working at the SCP Foundation
- but one thing forever changed: SCP-173 was

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:46.690
never underestimated again.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:54.629
Now go check out “SCP-173 Origin Story - How
173 Got to Site-19” and “The Eye Pods

00:17:54.629 --> 00:18:00.580
- SCP-131” for more terrifying incidents
involving this singularly savage statue!

